<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bonar Law</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1858">1858</a>–<a href="/years/1923">1923</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Andrew Bonar Law</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Andrew Bonar Law was a British Conservative politician, born in the colony of New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, who was prime minister of the United Kingdom from October 1922 to May 1923. Leader of the Conservatives from 1911, he opposed Irish Home Rule, served as chancellor of the exchequer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-lloyd-george">Lloyd George</a>'s wartime coalition, and resigned through ill health after only about 210 days in office.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bonar-law.jpg" alt="Bonar Law" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bain News Service · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bonar_Law_1923_portrait_by_Walter_Stoneman_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Andrew Bonar Law was born on 16 September 1858 in Kingston, now Five Rivers, New Brunswick, the son of the Reverend James Law, a minister of the Free Church of Scotland, and Eliza Kidston Law. His ancestry was Scottish and Irish, mainly Ulster Scots. He was named after Andrew Bonar, a biographer of the preacher Robert Murray McCheyne, whom his mother admired, and was always called Bonar by family and friends. He moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> in 1870, left school at sixteen to work in the iron industry, and became a partner in the iron merchant William Jacks' firm. He turned it into one of the most profitable in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a> and Scottish markets and was a wealthy man by thirty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Law won the Conservative seat of Glasgow Blackfriars and Hutchesontown in the general election of 1900, the khaki election, with a majority of 1,000, and moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. His first major speech, on 22 April 1902, concerned tariff reform: he argued for an imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/customs-union">customs union</a>. He lost his seat in 1906 but was returned at a by-election in Dulwich. After Joseph Chamberlain's stroke in 1906 he became the principal spokesman for tariff reform in Balfour's Shadow Cabinet. His wife died on 31 October 1909.</p><p>In 1911, after he was made a Privy Councillor, he became Conservative leader because the two front-runners, Walter Long and Austen Chamberlain, withdrew rather than risk splitting the party. He opposed the Third Home Rule Bill, and as the child of an Ulster family he believed the gap between Ulster Unionism and Irish Nationalism could never be crossed. On 30 July 1914 he agreed with H. H. Asquith to suspend the Home Rule issue for the war. He joined the Coalition Government as Secretary of State for the Colonies in May 1915, and later served as Chancellor of the Exchequer under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-lloyd-george">David Lloyd George</a>. Ill health forced his resignation as party leader in 1921.</p><p>By 1922 the coalition had become unpopular with Conservatives, who voted to end it. Law became leader again and prime minister, and won a clear majority at the 1922 general election. His brief premiership included negotiation with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s war loans.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Diagnosed with terminal throat cancer and unable to speak in Parliament, Law resigned on 20 May 1923, and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-v">George V</a> sent for Stanley Baldwin. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> later that year, aged 65. He was the first prime minister born outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> or Ireland and the shortest-serving of the 20th century, and the first to be cremated. At his funeral at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a>, Asquith remarked that they were burying the Unknown Prime Minister next to the Tomb of the Unknown Soldier, a phrase that gave the title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-blake">Robert Blake</a>'s biography.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bonar Law” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1922 the state of about 44 million people, under King George V and the Conservative Prime Minister <b>Andrew Bonar Law</b>, still ruled the largest empire in history, held mandates over Palestine, Transjordan and Iraq, possessed the world's largest navy alongside that of the United States and was…</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom">United Kingdom · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Rexton, in what is now <a href="/countries/canada">Canada</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.64,-64.87&amp;year=1900" aria-label="Rexton on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3024 524 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Bonar Law"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3474 794h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3474 794h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46.64,-64.87&amp;year=1900">What was here in 1900? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
